Object.assign(window.deepArticles, {
  "okinawa-prefecture":okinawaRyukyuArticle({
    title:"Okinawa Prefecture",infoboxKicker:"Japanese prefecture in the central and southern Ryukyus",
    lead:"Okinawa Prefecture is Japan's southwestern prefecture, extending from the Okinawa Islands through Miyako and Yaeyama to Yonaguni. Created from the former Ryukyu Domain in 1879, it remains under uninterrupted Japanese administration after Japan avoids war with the United States and therefore experiences neither the Battle of Okinawa nor American occupation.",
    canon:"The 1879 prefectural creation, Naha capital, island geography, former-kingdom inheritance, and Japanese annexation are historical. The absence of American invasion, occupation, reversion, and American base society follows the setting's divergence. Continued Japanese strategic administration, cultural preservation, and language shift are direct extrapolations; post-1941 governors, electoral history, population, district boundaries, and defense installations remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"geography",title:"Geography",html:"<p>The prefecture consists of separated island groups across the East China Sea and Philippine Sea. <a href='okinawa-island.html'>Okinawa Island</a> contains the greatest population and administrative concentration; Miyako and Yaeyama form the more distant southern districts, while Yonaguni lies close to Taiwan.</p>"},
      {id:"kingdom",title:"From kingdom to prefecture",html:"<p>The islands formed the core of the <a href='ryukyu-kingdom.html'>Ryukyu Kingdom</a>. The Meiji government converted the kingdom into Ryukyu Domain in 1872 and abolished it through the <a href='ryukyu-disposition.html'>Ryukyu Disposition</a> of 1879, establishing Okinawa Prefecture and removing the Shō court to Tokyo.</p>"},
      {id:"imperial",title:"Government under imperial Japan",html:"<p>Prefectural offices, police, courts, schools, conscription, taxation, transport, and national institutions steadily integrate the islands. Formal prefectural status does not prevent discrimination against Ryukyuan speech, ancestry, religion, or custom, and metropolitan officials long treat local difference as backwardness to be corrected.</p>"},
      {id:"war",title:"The war that does not reach Okinawa",html:"<p>Japan fights Britain and the European colonial powers after 1941 but follows the <a href='imperial-limited-war-directive.html'>limited-war settlement</a> that avoids attack on American territory. Okinawa is mobilized and fortified, yet no American landing destroys its cities, population, archives, or royal monuments.</p>"},
      {id:"postwar",title:"Uninterrupted postwar administration",html:"<p>There is no American military government, separate currency, 1972 reversion, or vast American base economy. Japanese bases and transport commands remain important, but civil development proceeds from institutions already operating before 1947 rather than from reconstruction under a foreign occupation.</p>"},
      {id:"economy",title:"Economy and communications",html:"<p>Shipping, aviation, fisheries, sugar, agriculture, tourism, construction, public employment, and military logistics connect the prefecture to Kyūshū, Taiwan, the Philippines, and the South Seas. Naha is the principal gateway, while island economies remain vulnerable to typhoons, distance, and freight cost.</p>"},
      {id:"culture",title:"Language and cultural policy",html:"<p>Japanese dominates education and public advancement. <a href='okinawan-language.html'>Okinawan</a>, <a href='kunigami-language.html'>Kunigami</a>, <a href='miyakoan-language.html'>Miyakoan</a>, <a href='yaeyama-language.html'>Yaeyama</a>, and <a href='yonaguni-language.html'>Yonaguni</a> survive unevenly. Preservationism funds archives and instruction without recognizing a separate Ryukyuan nationality.</p>"},
      {id:"post-preservation",title:"Administration after preservationism",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-j-20260816-r9k3d writer=j kind=analysis created=2026-08-16T12:42:26-06:00 --><p>Okinawa's prefectural and municipal offices administered schools, public health, welfare, local roads and harbors, utilities, fisheries, construction, and typhoon relief through the same public-law hierarchy used elsewhere in Japan. National courts, taxation, conscription, defense commands, strategic ports, and long-distance transport standards connected the islands directly to Tokyo. Distance gave Naha and the island districts practical discretion over delivery, while nationality, defense, and the constitutional position of the prefecture remained national questions.</p><p>Post-preservationist governments kept archives, language classes, museums, religious custodians, performance schools, and the surviving institutions of Shuri within this prefectural settlement. Recognition of Ryukyuan history no longer implied restoration of the kingdom, a separate nationality, or government organized by ancestry. Administrative continuity delivered services and preserved unusually intact cultural assets. It also rested on the coercive annexation of 1879, generations of language displacement and discrimination, military claims on land, and a national government that excluded secession from legitimate bargaining.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"1985",title:"Okinawa Prefecture in 1985",html:"<p>By 1985 Okinawa is legally ordinary Japanese territory and strategically the hinge between the home islands and the southern empire. Its society is more continuously Japanized than in real-world history, but its historic fabric is less physically shattered and its former-kingdom institutions provide stronger material foundations for cultural recovery.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"okinawa-island.html",kicker:"Principal island",label:"Okinawa Island"},{href:"naha.html",kicker:"Prefectural capital",label:"Naha"},{href:"shuri.html",kicker:"Former royal capital",label:"Shuri"},{href:"ryukyu-kingdom.html",kicker:"Predecessor state",label:"Ryukyu Kingdom"},{href:"ryukyuan-languages.html",kicker:"Language family",label:"Ryukyuan Languages"},{href:"ryukyu-islands.html",kicker:"Wider archipelago",label:"Ryukyu Islands"}],
    facts:[["Country","Empire of Japan"],["Established","1879"],["Capital","Naha"],["Principal island","Okinawa Island"],["Southern districts","Miyako and Yaeyama"],["American occupation","None"],["Nationality","Japanese"],["Status in 1985","Prefecture of integral Japan"]],
    sources:[{href:"https://www.pref.okinawa.jp/bunkakoryu/bunkageijutsu/1009556/1009653/1009658/1002577/1024285/index.html",label:"Okinawa Prefecture — history of Okinawa"},{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Okinawa_Prefecture",label:"Historical and geographical reference — Okinawa Prefecture"}]
  }),

  "okinawa-island":okinawaRyukyuArticle({
    title:"Okinawa Island",category:"Islands of Japan",infoboxKicker:"Largest island of Okinawa Prefecture",
    lead:"Okinawa Island is the largest and most populous island of Okinawa Prefecture and the political center of the former Ryukyu Kingdom. Naha and Shuri occupy its urbanized south, central districts form the principal transport and military belt, and the forested Yanbaru north remains culturally and ecologically distinct.",
    canon:"The island's geography, Naha–Shuri urban core, Yanbaru north, kingdom history, and pre-1941 Japanese administration are historical. The absence of the Battle of Okinawa and American occupation is fixed by the setting. The resulting Japanese base geography, uninterrupted urban growth, and preservation history are extrapolated; exact 1985 population, municipalities, railways, installations, and land use remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"geography",title:"Physical geography",html:"<p>The island is long and narrow. Limestone plateaus and densely settled plains characterize much of the south, while the northern Yanbaru region is hillier, more forested, and less urban. Reefs, narrow coastal shelves, typhoons, drought, and limited flat land shape settlement and engineering.</p>"},
      {id:"political",title:"Political center of the kingdom",html:"<p>Competing Okinawan polities were unified under the Shō dynasty in the fifteenth century. <a href='shuri.html'>Shuri</a> became the royal and ceremonial capital, while <a href='naha.html'>Naha</a> served as the principal port and commercial gateway.</p>"},
      {id:"annexation",title:"Japanese incorporation",html:"<p>After Satsuma's 1609 conquest, the kingdom remained under dual Chinese tributary and Japanese feudal constraints. Meiji annexation in 1879 made the island the administrative core of Okinawa Prefecture. Schools, conscription, migration, and markets linked it more tightly to the home islands.</p>"},
      {id:"war",title:"Strategic mobilization without invasion",html:"<p>Airfields, coastal defenses, depots, and garrisons expand during the long war. Because Japan never attacks the United States, the island is not invaded in 1945. The population avoids the mass civilian death and physical annihilation that would have accompanied a battle through Naha, Shuri, and the southern ridge lines.</p>"},
      {id:"bases",title:"Japanese defense geography",html:"<p>Postwar installations belong to the Japanese armed services rather than to a foreign occupying power. They protect routes toward Taiwan, the Philippines, and the South Seas. Land disputes and military accidents remain local political issues, but there is no American-base sovereignty question.</p>"},
      {id:"economy",title:"Urban and rural economy",html:"<p>The Naha metropolitan area concentrates government, port activity, education, retail, communications, and services. Central districts host industry and defense logistics. Northern communities depend more on forestry, farming, fishing, small ports, and later nature tourism.</p>"},
      {id:"language",title:"Language geography",html:"<p>Central and southern communities historically speak Okinawan varieties; Yanbaru communities speak <a href='kunigami-language.html'>Kunigami</a> varieties. Standard Japanese becomes dominant through school, military service, broadcasting, administration, and urban migration, while Okinawan Japanese develops as the vigorous everyday contact variety.</p>"},
      {id:"1985",title:"Okinawa Island in 1985",html:"<p>By 1985 the island is a dense Japanese regional center whose older cities and monuments retain more continuous physical fabric than elsewhere in possible wartime histories. Preservation policy uses that survival to support heritage, even as ordinary family transmission of the older languages continues to weaken.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"okinawa-prefecture.html",kicker:"Prefecture",label:"Okinawa Prefecture"},{href:"naha.html",kicker:"Principal city",label:"Naha"},{href:"shuri.html",kicker:"Royal center",label:"Shuri"},{href:"okinawan-language.html",kicker:"Southern language",label:"Okinawan Language"},{href:"kunigami-language.html",kicker:"Northern language",label:"Kunigami Language"},{href:"taiwan.html",kicker:"Southwestern neighbor",label:"Taiwan"}],
    facts:[["Archipelago","Ryukyu Islands"],["Prefecture","Okinawa"],["Principal city","Naha"],["Former capital","Shuri"],["Northern region","Yanbaru"],["Battle of Okinawa","Does not occur"],["Foreign occupation","None"],["Status in 1985","Strategic and demographic center of the prefecture"]],
    sources:[{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Okinawa_Island",label:"Geographical reference — Okinawa Island"}]
  }),

  "naha":okinawaRyukyuArticle({
    title:"Naha",category:"Cities of Imperial Japan",infoboxKicker:"Capital of Okinawa Prefecture",
    lead:"Naha is the capital, largest city, principal port, and administrative center of Okinawa Prefecture. It developed as the maritime gateway of the Ryukyu Kingdom below the royal capital at Shuri and became the prefectural capital after annexation. In the setting it avoids wartime destruction and grows through continuous Japanese rule.",
    canon:"Naha's port function, kingdom-era commercial role, prefectural capital status, 1921 city establishment, and relation to Shuri are historical. Survival without the 1944 air raids, 1945 battle, or American occupation follows the setting. Postwar metropolitan growth, port expansion, and imperial-route role are extrapolated; exact population, municipal mergers, mayors, and infrastructure chronology remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"port",title:"Kingdom port",html:"<p>Naha connected the Shuri court to China, Japan, Korea, and Southeast Asian trade. Envoys, merchants, interpreters, ship crews, warehouses, temples, and foreign communities made the port economically more outward-facing than the royal capital above it.</p>"},
      {id:"prefecture",title:"Prefectural capital",html:"<p>After the <a href='ryukyu-disposition.html'>1879 annexation</a>, Japanese offices and commercial institutions concentrate in Naha. The modern municipality becomes a city in 1921 and absorbs surrounding urban districts as the Naha–Shuri conurbation expands.</p>"},
      {id:"harbor",title:"Harbor and transport",html:"<p>Breakwaters, wharves, ferries, coastal shipping, warehouses, customs, aviation links, and roads make Naha the transfer point between the home islands and the scattered prefecture. Military and civil shipping share a harbor whose operation is vital after every typhoon.</p>"},
      {id:"war",title:"Continuity through the long war",html:"<p>Naha is mobilized and exposed to maritime danger but never subjected to the American air campaign and ground assault of 1944–45. Government records, neighborhoods, temples, family property, and commercial institutions therefore survive in much greater continuity.</p>"},
      {id:"metropolis",title:"Naha–Shuri metropolis",html:"<p>Urban growth joins Naha's administrative and commercial districts to <a href='shuri.html'>Shuri's</a> schools, shrines, former aristocratic neighborhoods, and cultural institutions. The two centers retain distinct historical identities inside one metropolitan economy.</p>"},
      {id:"imperial",title:"Southwestern imperial gateway",html:"<p>Routes to Taiwan, the Philippines, Hong Kong, the South Seas, and the Japanese home islands give Naha an importance greater than its local market. Officials, sailors, soldiers, students, migrant workers, tourists, and corporate staff pass through the city.</p>"},
      {id:"culture",title:"Language and public culture",html:"<p>Standard Japanese dominates administration, schools, and metropolitan media. Okinawan Japanese marks regional public life, while older <a href='okinawan-language.html'>Okinawan</a> survives in theater, song, ritual, family speech, scholarship, and preservationist broadcasting.</p>"},
      {id:"1985",title:"Naha in 1985",html:"<p>By 1985 Naha is a mature regional capital rather than a city reconstructed after occupation. Its historic port, surviving urban fabric, imperial transport role, and modern public institutions support a confident Japanese civic identity alongside persistent Ryukyuan cultural particularity.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"okinawa-prefecture.html",kicker:"Prefecture",label:"Okinawa Prefecture"},{href:"okinawa-island.html",kicker:"Island",label:"Okinawa Island"},{href:"shuri.html",kicker:"Historic twin center",label:"Shuri"},{href:"ryukyu-kingdom.html",kicker:"Commercial predecessor",label:"Ryukyu Kingdom"},{href:"cities-of-imperial-japan.html",kicker:"Urban system",label:"Cities of Imperial Japan"}],
    facts:[["Country","Empire of Japan"],["Prefecture","Okinawa"],["Municipal status","City from 1921"],["Historical role","Port of the Ryukyu Kingdom"],["Modern role","Prefectural capital and principal gateway"],["Historic partner city","Shuri"],["Wartime destruction in 1945","None"],["Status in 1985","Southwestern imperial transport and administrative center"]],
    sources:[{href:"https://www.rekishi-archive.city.naha.okinawa.jp/en/history",label:"Naha City Museum of History — city and dynasty history"},{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Naha",label:"Historical and geographical reference — Naha"}]
  }),

  "shuri":okinawaRyukyuArticle({
    title:"Shuri",category:"Cities of Imperial Japan",infoboxKicker:"Former royal capital of the Ryukyu Kingdom",
    lead:"Shuri is the former capital and court city of the Ryukyu Kingdom, centered on Shuri Castle above Naha. Annexation ended its political sovereignty but not its concentration of royal sites, aristocratic memory, religion, education, crafts, and language. Because no Battle of Okinawa occurs, the castle city survives the war without its historical 1945 destruction.",
    canon:"Shuri's royal-capital role, castle, court society, Satsuma seizure, 1879 occupation, decline, 1921 city status, and prewar preservation are historical. The absence of American bombardment and ground battle follows setting canon. Survival of the prewar castle fabric and its postwar use as a preservationist center are direct extrapolations; fires, restoration projects, municipal status, institutions, and 1985 urban boundaries remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"capital",title:"Royal capital",html:"<p>Shō Hashi made Shuri the capital of the unified kingdom in the fifteenth century. The castle, court offices, aristocratic residences, temples, sacred sites, workshops, and ceremonial roads made it the political and cultural center for roughly four and a half centuries.</p>"},
      {id:"satsuma",title:"Satsuma seizure",html:"<p>Satsuma forces captured Shuri Castle in 1609 and compelled King Shō Nei to accept subordination. The court returned and continued governing, but its diplomacy, tribute, trade, and taxation operated under Japanese feudal constraint.</p>"},
      {id:"annexation",title:"Loss of the court",html:"<p>Japanese forces occupied the castle during the <a href='ryukyu-disposition.html'>Ryukyu Disposition</a> of 1879. King Shō Tai left for Tokyo, noble incomes contracted, and Naha replaced Shuri as the modern administrative center. Former court families dispersed or entered education, bureaucracy, and cultural work.</p>"},
      {id:"preservation",title:"Prewar preservation",html:"<p>Local and metropolitan scholars resisted demolition and neglect. Restoration work and national-treasure protection in the 1920s preserved the castle as a historic and religious monument, though imperial institutions reframed the royal palace within Japanese national history.</p>"},
      {id:"survival",title:"Survival of Shuri Castle",html:"<p>The island is never invaded by the United States, so artillery and ground fighting do not level the castle, royal mausoleum, gates, shrines, and surrounding city in 1945. Wartime military use still creates risk, but physical continuity transforms postwar heritage politics.</p>"},
      {id:"education",title:"Education and cultural institutions",html:"<p>Schools, archives, museums, religious custodians, music and dance organizations, textile workshops, and language scholars gather around the former capital. Shuri becomes the strongest institutional base for Ryukyuan cultural preservation inside Japan proper.</p>"},
      {id:"language",title:"Shuri speech",html:"<p>The prestigious Shuri variety of <a href='okinawan-language.html'>Okinawan</a> preserves court vocabulary and performance traditions. It supplies material for dictionaries and teaching standards but cannot represent every island variety without controversy.</p>"},
      {id:"1985",title:"Shuri in 1985",html:"<p>By 1985 Shuri forms part of metropolitan Naha while retaining a distinct civic name and landscape. The intact castle complex is a ceremonial site, museum, educational center, and symbol through which preservationists reconcile Ryukyuan historical difference with Japanese nationality.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"naha.html",kicker:"Modern metropolitan center",label:"Naha"},{href:"ryukyu-kingdom.html",kicker:"Former state",label:"Ryukyu Kingdom"},{href:"ryukyu-disposition.html",kicker:"End of sovereignty",label:"Ryukyu Disposition"},{href:"okinawan-language.html",kicker:"Court and regional language",label:"Okinawan Language"},{href:"japanese-preservationism.html",kicker:"Cultural policy",label:"Japanese Preservationism"}],
    facts:[["Island","Okinawa"],["Historical role","Royal capital"],["Principal monument","Shuri Castle"],["Capital of unified kingdom","From 1429"],["Annexation","1879"],["Separate city status","1921 historically; later metropolitan status open"],["Destruction in 1945","Does not occur"],["Status in 1985","Historic and cultural district of metropolitan Naha"]],
    sources:[{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shuri%2C_Okinawa",label:"Historical reference — Shuri"},{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shuri_Castle",label:"Architectural reference — Shuri Castle"}]
  }),

  "ryukyu-kingdom":okinawaRyukyuArticle({
    title:"Ryukyu Kingdom",category:"Former countries",eyebrow:"Former monarchy · 1429–1879",infoboxKicker:"Predecessor state of Okinawa Prefecture",
    lead:"The Ryukyu Kingdom was the island monarchy ruled from Shuri between 1429 and 1879. It joined Okinawa, the southern islands, and for periods the Amami chain into a maritime state whose court traded and exchanged tribute across East and Southeast Asia. After Satsuma conquered it in 1609, the kingdom survived under dual Chinese tributary and Japanese feudal constraints until Meiji annexation.",
    canon:"The kingdom's chronology, Shō monarchy, maritime commerce, Chinese tributary relations, 1609 Satsuma invasion, dual subordination, and 1879 abolition are historical. Its place in the setting after 1941 is a matter of memory, scholarship, religion, heritage, and regional politics inside victorious imperial Japan. Exact postwar historiographic schools, museums, royal-house roles, and restoration policy remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"unification",title:"Unification under Shō Hashi",html:"<p>Shō Hashi unified the competing Okinawan kingdoms of Hokuzan, Chūzan, and Nanzan in 1429 and established the first Shō dynasty. <a href='shuri.html'>Shuri</a> became the court capital and Naha the maritime gateway.</p>"},
      {id:"trade",title:"Maritime trade state",html:"<p>Royal missions and licensed merchants connected the islands with Ming China, Japan, Korea, Siam, Malacca, and other ports. Tribute and exchange supplied prestige goods, revenue, diplomatic status, and the cosmopolitan culture of court and harbor.</p>"},
      {id:"islands",title:"Island administration",html:"<p>The court governed through appointed officials, local elites, tribute obligations, ritual authority, and maritime routes. Okinawa formed the center, while Amami, Miyako, Yaeyama, and other islands experienced different degrees of supervision and resistance.</p>"},
      {id:"satsuma",title:"Invasion of 1609",html:"<p>Satsuma Domain invaded in 1609, defeated royal forces, occupied Shuri, and subordinated the kingdom. The Shō monarch returned to rule, but Satsuma controlled essential foreign relations, tribute, taxation, and trade while preserving a façade useful for commerce with China.</p>"},
      {id:"dual",title:"Dual subordination",html:"<p>The kingdom continued tributary missions to the Ming and Qing courts while answering to Satsuma and the Tokugawa order. This layered position allowed diplomatic usefulness but imposed extraction and compelled the court to conceal the full extent of Japanese control.</p>"},
      {id:"abolition",title:"Abolition and annexation",html:"<p>Tokyo declared Ryukyu Domain in 1872 and abolished the monarchy in 1879. The <a href='ryukyu-disposition.html'>Ryukyu Disposition</a> removed the last king, established Okinawa Prefecture, and transformed foreign-relations disputes into questions of Japanese domestic administration.</p>"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Institutional and cultural legacy",html:"<p>Royal ritual, sacred sites, tombs, court music, dance, textiles, ceramics, martial traditions, genealogies, diplomatic records, and island languages preserve a history not reducible to ordinary prefectural development.</p>"},
      {id:"memory",title:"Memory in imperial Japan",html:"<p>Official history calls the kingdom an ancestral regional polity fulfilled through incorporation. Ryukyuan scholars, clergy, and preservationists distinguish cultural inheritance from consent to annexation. The state permits the argument so long as it does not become a claim to foreign sovereignty.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"shuri.html",kicker:"Royal capital",label:"Shuri"},{href:"naha.html",kicker:"Kingdom port",label:"Naha"},{href:"ryukyu-disposition.html",kicker:"Abolition",label:"Ryukyu Disposition"},{href:"amami-islands.html",kicker:"Northern tributary region",label:"Amami Islands"},{href:"ryukyuan-people.html",kicker:"Cultural descendants",label:"Ryukyuan People"},{href:"ryukyu-islands.html",kicker:"Territorial setting",label:"Ryukyu Islands"}],
    facts:[["Period","1429–1879"],["Capital","Shuri"],["Ruling house","Shō dynasty"],["Principal port","Naha"],["Chinese relationship","Tributary kingdom"],["Satsuma conquest","1609"],["Immediate successor","Ryukyu Domain, then Okinawa Prefecture"],["Abolished","1879"]],
    sources:[{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ryukyu_Kingdom",label:"Historical reference — Ryukyu Kingdom"},{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Invasion_of_Ryukyu",label:"Historical reference — Satsuma invasion of 1609"}]
  }),

  "ryukyu-disposition":okinawaRyukyuArticle({
    title:"Ryukyu Disposition",category:"Political history",eyebrow:"Annexation of Ryukyu · 1872–1879",infoboxKicker:"Abolition of the Ryukyu Kingdom",
    lead:"The Ryukyu Disposition was the Meiji government's process of abolishing the Ryukyu Kingdom and incorporating its territory as Okinawa Prefecture. It began with the creation of Ryukyu Domain in 1872 and culminated in March 1879 when Japanese troops and police occupied Shuri, removed the Shō government, and established direct prefectural administration.",
    canon:"The 1872 domain conversion, diplomatic pressure, 1879 military-police mission, abolition, removal of Shō Tai, prefectural creation, and Chinese dispute are historical. In-setting interpretation is extrapolated through Japanese preservationism and the doctrine of cultural nationality; terminology, commemorations, reparative measures, and regional political positions remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"background",title:"Background",html:"<p>The kingdom had lived under Satsuma control while continuing formal tributary relations with China since 1609. Meiji state-building rejected that ambiguity and sought one sovereign jurisdiction, taxation system, school order, and foreign policy across the islands.</p>"},
      {id:"domain",title:"Ryukyu Domain, 1872",html:"<p>Tokyo redesignated the kingdom as Ryukyu Domain and made Shō Tai a domain king within the Japanese peerage order. The measure asserted Japanese sovereignty while initially retaining parts of the court administration.</p>"},
      {id:"diplomacy",title:"End of dual diplomacy",html:"<p>The government ordered the court to end tributary relations and independent missions to Qing China. Ryukyuan officials petitioned for continuation or compromise, while Chinese officials disputed Japan's unilateral settlement.</p>"},
      {id:"1879",title:"Abolition in 1879",html:"<p>Matsuda Michiyuki arrived with police and troops, announced the abolition of the domain, occupied <a href='shuri.html'>Shuri Castle</a>, and established Okinawa Prefecture. Shō Tai was compelled to relocate to Tokyo.</p>"},
      {id:"institutions",title:"Institutional transformation",html:"<p>Prefectural government displaced royal offices. Land, taxation, policing, schooling, courts, conscription, titles, and local administration were reorganized unevenly. Some older offices and village structures survived temporarily because immediate replacement was impractical.</p>"},
      {id:"society",title:"Social consequences",html:"<p>Former aristocratic households lost offices, stipends, dependents, and political purpose. Commoners faced new national institutions and economic pressures. Migration and Japanese schooling accelerated while attachment to court ritual and local communities continued.</p>"},
      {id:"historiography",title:"Annexation or national unification",html:"<p>Imperial histories traditionally describe the process as final unification with Japan. Ryukyuan scholarship also uses annexation, disposal, or colonization to emphasize coercion and the destruction of a sovereign court. Preservationist institutions permit competing descriptions within limits.</p>"},
      {id:"1985",title:"Public meaning by 1985",html:"<p>By 1985 the territorial result is not politically negotiable. The dispute concerns language, ceremony, archives, education, land, and whether acknowledging coercive annexation requires institutional autonomy. Tokyo accepts cultural reappraisal while rejecting a right of secession.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"ryukyu-kingdom.html",kicker:"Abolished state",label:"Ryukyu Kingdom"},{href:"okinawa-prefecture.html",kicker:"Successor administration",label:"Okinawa Prefecture"},{href:"shuri.html",kicker:"Site of abolition",label:"Shuri"},{href:"ryukyuan-people.html",kicker:"Affected people",label:"Ryukyuan People"},{href:"japanese-preservationism.html",kicker:"Later reinterpretation",label:"Japanese Preservationism"}],
    facts:[["Period","1872–1879"],["Predecessor","Ryukyu Kingdom"],["Intermediate status","Ryukyu Domain"],["Final act","March 1879"],["Imperial commissioner","Matsuda Michiyuki"],["Last king","Shō Tai"],["Successor","Okinawa Prefecture"],["Political result in 1985","Accepted by the state; historical interpretation contested"]],
    sources:[{href:"https://www.rekishi-archive.city.naha.okinawa.jp/en/history",label:"Naha City Museum of History — annexation chronology"},{href:"https://rca.open.ed.jp/web_e/history/story/epoch4/syobun_6.html",label:"Okinawa history archive — abolition of the kingdom"},{href:"https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/jjcanth/70/4/70_KJ00004582410/_article/-char/en",label:"Historical anthropology reference — Ryukyu annexation"}]
  }),

  "amami-islands":okinawaRyukyuArticle({
    title:"Amami Islands",category:"Islands of Japan",infoboxKicker:"Northern Ryukyuan archipelago in Kagoshima Prefecture",
    lead:"The Amami Islands are the northern Ryukyuan archipelago between Kyūshū and Okinawa. Historically drawn into the Ryukyu Kingdom and conquered by Satsuma in 1609, they remain administratively part of Kagoshima Prefecture rather than Okinawa. Their languages and cultural institutions make them a bridge—and often a boundary—between mainland Japanese and Okinawan regional identities.",
    canon:"The geography, Ryukyu relationship, 1609 Satsuma conquest, direct Satsuma control, sugar economy, Kagoshima administration, and linguistic distinctiveness are historical. The absence of post-1945 American occupation and 1953 reversion follows the setting. Mature imperial development and preservation policy are extrapolated; exact wartime administration, postwar industries, population, and language status remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"geography",title:"Island chain",html:"<p>Amami Ōshima, Kikai, Tokunoshima, Okinoerabu, Yoron, and smaller islands form the chain between the Tokara and Okinawa groups. Reefs, subtropical forests, typhoons, limited farmland, and maritime distance produce strongly local island societies.</p>"},
      {id:"ryukyu",title:"Relations with the Ryukyu Kingdom",html:"<p>Amami communities paid tribute to or came under the influence of the Ryukyu court at different times. Their northern location also tied them to Kyūshū trade, raiding, migration, and political pressure.</p>"},
      {id:"satsuma",title:"Satsuma conquest",html:"<p>Satsuma seized the islands during its 1609 invasion and placed Amami under more direct control than the remaining kingdom. The islands became an administrative and commercial hinge through which Satsuma supervised Ryukyu.</p>"},
      {id:"sugar",title:"Sugar and extraction",html:"<p>Sugar production, tribute, debt, merchants, and monopoly institutions transformed land and labor. The sugar regime enriched Satsuma while leaving a durable memory of extraction and distinguishing Amami's experience from both Okinawa and mainland Kagoshima.</p>"},
      {id:"modern",title:"Modern Japanese administration",html:"<p>Meiji government incorporated the islands through Kagoshima Prefecture. Schools, conscription, migration, shipping, and national markets strengthened Japanese identity, while village life and island speech retained Ryukyuan continuities.</p>"},
      {id:"war",title:"No American separation",html:"<p>Because Japan does not fight the United States, Amami is never detached into an American occupation zone after 1945 and never undergoes a 1953 reversion. Administrative and monetary continuity with Kagoshima therefore remains stronger.</p>"},
      {id:"language",title:"Amami languages",html:"<p>Amami varieties form the northernmost branch of the Ryukyuan languages. They differ sharply among islands and communities. Japanese schooling and outward migration weaken transmission, while classification as dialects obscures their linguistic distance from standard Japanese.</p>"},
      {id:"1985",title:"Amami in 1985",html:"<p>By 1985 Amami is Japanese in nationality and Kagoshima administration but irreducibly northern Ryukyuan in much cultural history. Preservation programs support speech, song, ritual, textiles, ecology, and local archives without moving the islands into Okinawa Prefecture.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"ryukyu-islands.html",kicker:"Wider archipelago",label:"Ryukyu Islands"},{href:"ryukyu-kingdom.html",kicker:"Former southern authority",label:"Ryukyu Kingdom"},{href:"ryukyuan-people.html",kicker:"Regional identity",label:"Ryukyuan People"},{href:"ryukyuan-languages.html",kicker:"Language family",label:"Ryukyuan Languages"},{href:"okinawa-prefecture.html",kicker:"Southern prefecture",label:"Okinawa Prefecture"}],
    facts:[["Country","Empire of Japan"],["Prefecture","Kagoshima"],["Principal island","Amami Ōshima"],["Historical affiliation","Ryukyu Kingdom"],["Satsuma conquest","1609"],["Major historical commodity","Sugar"],["American occupation","None"],["Status in 1985","Japanese islands with northern Ryukyuan cultural identity"]],
    sources:[{href:"https://www.mlit.go.jp/tagengo-db/en/R2-00848.html",label:"Japan Tourism Agency — history of Amami Ōshima"},{href:"https://amami-horizon.com/en/culture/history/amami-modern",label:"Amami historical reference — early modern and modern archipelago"},{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amami_Islands",label:"Geographical reference — Amami Islands"}]
  }),

  "okinawan-language":okinawaRyukyuArticle({
    title:"Okinawan language",category:"Languages",infoboxKicker:"Northern Ryukyuan language of central and southern Okinawa",
    lead:"Okinawan is the Northern Ryukyuan language historically spoken across central and southern Okinawa Island and neighboring islands. The Shuri–Naha variety carries the strongest court and literary prestige. Imperial schools classify it as a dialect and make standard Japanese the language of advancement; by 1985 Okinawan survives as an endangered community and cultural language rather than the normal speech of most children.",
    canon:"Okinawan's Japonic classification, Shuri–Naha prestige, local variation, suppression through Japanese schooling, and historical decline are baseline. The absence of American occupation and the setting's 1970s preservationist movement alter its later trajectory. Exact speaker numbers, endangerment label, standardized orthography, school provision, and broadcasting remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"classification",title:"Classification",html:"<p>Okinawan belongs to the Northern Ryukyuan branch of Japonic. It is related to Japanese but is not mutually intelligible with standard Japanese in ordinary traditional speech. It is also distinct from <a href='kunigami-language.html'>Kunigami</a> in northern Okinawa.</p>"},
      {id:"varieties",title:"Regional varieties",html:"<p>Communities across the central and southern island maintain local phonology, vocabulary, grammar, and performance forms. The Shuri–Naha variety gains special visibility because of the former court, port, theater, scholarship, and urban publishing.</p>"},
      {id:"court",title:"Court and literary register",html:"<p>Shuri speech preserves vocabulary connected to rank, ceremony, diplomacy, music, dance, religion, and palace life. Songs, drama, genealogies, diplomatic records, and oral performance transmit layers not captured by everyday modern conversation.</p>"},
      {id:"school",title:"Dialect policy and language shift",html:"<p>Schools demand standard Japanese and stigmatize local speech. Administrative life, conscription, metropolitan employment, radio, and inter-island migration make Japanese materially necessary. Calling Okinawan a dialect turns replacement into national education rather than visible language loss.</p>"},
      {id:"continuity",title:"Uninterrupted Japanese rule",html:"<p>There is no American occupation government or separate postwar public sphere. Japanese institutions continue directly through 1947, accelerating assimilation. At the same time, the survival of Shuri, archives, neighborhoods, and cultural specialists leaves more original material for later recovery.</p>"},
      {id:"contact",title:"Okinawan Japanese",html:"<p>Regional Japanese absorbs Okinawan pronunciation, vocabulary, discourse habits, names, and cultural references. This Okinawan Japanese becomes vigorous urban speech, but preservationists distinguish it from the older language whose grammar and lexicon cannot be recovered merely from an accent.</p>"},
      {id:"preservation",title:"Preservation movement",html:"<p>From the 1970s, dictionaries, recordings, theater, song, local radio, school clubs, elder programs, and university work gain support. Debate divides advocates of classical Shuri prestige, community varieties, and a simplified teaching standard.</p>"},
      {id:"1985",title:"Status in 1985",html:"<p>By 1985 fluent speakers are concentrated among older generations and cultural specialists. Public recognition has improved faster than household transmission. The language is clearly endangered, though its exact official classification remains deliberately open.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"ryukyuan-languages.html",kicker:"Language family",label:"Ryukyuan Languages"},{href:"shuri.html",kicker:"Prestige center",label:"Shuri"},{href:"naha.html",kicker:"Urban center",label:"Naha"},{href:"okinawa-island.html",kicker:"Principal homeland",label:"Okinawa Island"},{href:"japanese-preservationism.html",kicker:"Revival policy",label:"Japanese Preservationism"}],
    facts:[["Family","Japonic"],["Branch","Northern Ryukyuan"],["Principal region","Central and southern Okinawa Island"],["Prestige variety","Shuri–Naha"],["Writing","Japanese scripts and modern teaching orthographies"],["Dominant contact language","Japanese"],["American occupation influence","Absent"],["Status in 1985","Endangered; exact formal label open"]],
    sources:[{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Okinawan_language",label:"Linguistic reference — Okinawan language"},{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ryukyuan_languages",label:"Comparative reference — Ryukyuan languages"}]
  }),

  "kunigami-language":okinawaRyukyuArticle({
    title:"Kunigami language",category:"Languages",infoboxKicker:"Northern Ryukyuan language of Yanbaru",
    lead:"Kunigami, also called Northern Okinawan or Yanbaru speech, is the Northern Ryukyuan language of northern Okinawa Island and several nearby communities. Its varieties center on Kunigami District, Nago, Nakijin, and the forested Yanbaru region. It remains distinct from central and southern Okinawan despite long official treatment as a local dialect.",
    canon:"Kunigami's region, Northern Ryukyuan classification, Nakijin prestige in description, community variation, and decline under Japanese language policy are historical baseline. Uninterrupted imperial schooling and later preservationism follow setting canon. Exact 1985 speaker count, education, orthography, and endangerment designation remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"region",title:"Yanbaru homeland",html:"<p>Kunigami varieties are spoken in northern Okinawa Island, particularly Kunigami District, Nago, Nakijin, and the Yanbaru region. Smaller neighboring islands preserve related local speech shaped by maritime contact and village isolation.</p>"},
      {id:"classification",title:"Classification",html:"<p>Kunigami belongs to Northern Ryukyuan and is closely related to, but distinct from, <a href='okinawan-language.html'>Okinawan</a>. Boundaries differ among linguistic classifications because neighboring island and village varieties form a complex continuum.</p>"},
      {id:"nakijin",title:"Nakijin and descriptive prestige",html:"<p>The Nakijin variety is frequently used in dictionaries and linguistic description. Its visibility reflects scholarly documentation rather than political authority over every northern community.</p>"},
      {id:"local",title:"Community varieties",html:"<p>Speakers traditionally identify speech by village, island, district, or style rather than by one standardized Kunigami nation-language. Local pronunciation and vocabulary can be powerful markers of origin even among people who use Japanese publicly.</p>"},
      {id:"shift",title:"Shift to Japanese",html:"<p>Schools, military service, government employment, roads, radio, and migration toward Naha or the home islands make standard Japanese dominant. Children increasingly learn Japanese or regional Japanese first, leaving Kunigami to older household and ceremonial domains.</p>"},
      {id:"war",title:"Continuity without occupation",html:"<p>Northern Okinawa avoids the American invasion and occupation that might otherwise reshape institutions and contact vocabulary. Japanese-language continuity is stronger, while villages and cultural records suffer less wartime physical destruction.</p>"},
      {id:"preservation",title:"Documentation and revival",html:"<p>Local dictionaries, recordings, folk songs, place-names, ecological vocabulary, school clubs, and elder teaching form the principal preservation tools. A standardized teaching form is useful but risks treating Nakijin or another documented variety as the whole language.</p>"},
      {id:"1985",title:"Status in 1985",html:"<p>By 1985 Kunigami is endangered and no longer the automatic language of northern children. It retains stronger symbolic attachment to Yanbaru place and ancestry than routine public use. Exact speaker numbers and formal status remain open.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"okinawan-language.html",kicker:"Southern neighbor",label:"Okinawan Language"},{href:"okinawa-island.html",kicker:"Homeland",label:"Okinawa Island"},{href:"ryukyuan-languages.html",kicker:"Language family",label:"Ryukyuan Languages"},{href:"okinawa-prefecture.html",kicker:"Administration",label:"Okinawa Prefecture"},{href:"japanese-preservationism.html",kicker:"Revival context",label:"Japanese Preservationism"}],
    facts:[["Family","Japonic"],["Branch","Northern Ryukyuan"],["Principal region","Yanbaru and northern Okinawa Island"],["Descriptive center","Nakijin"],["Related language","Okinawan"],["Dominant contact language","Japanese"],["Traditional identity scale","Village and district"],["Status in 1985","Endangered; exact speaker count open"]],
    sources:[{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kunigami_language",label:"Linguistic reference — Kunigami language"}]
  }),

  "miyakoan-language":okinawaRyukyuArticle({
    title:"Miyakoan language",category:"Languages",infoboxKicker:"Southern Ryukyuan language of the Miyako Islands",
    lead:"Miyakoan is the Southern Ryukyuan language of Miyako Island and its neighboring islands. It is most closely related to Yaeyama among the major named languages, yet its island varieties are internally diverse. Japanese schooling, migration, military service, broadcasting, and inter-island commerce place it under severe pressure by 1985.",
    canon:"Miyakoan's Southern Ryukyuan classification, geography, relation to Yaeyama, internal diversity, and real-world endangerment are baseline. The setting removes American occupation while preserving Japanese rule and later cultural programs. Exact dialect map, speaker population, formal label, orthography, and school provision remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"islands",title:"Miyako Islands",html:"<p>Miyako Island is the largest population center, with related varieties on Ikema, Irabu, Tarama, and other islands. Shallow soils, drought, typhoons, reefs, and maritime distance shape specialized vocabulary and strong settlement identities.</p>"},
      {id:"classification",title:"Southern Ryukyuan classification",html:"<p>Miyakoan belongs to the Southern Ryukyuan branch of Japonic. It is related to Yaeyama and Yonaguni but is not simply a southern dialect of standard Japanese or of Okinawan.</p>"},
      {id:"varieties",title:"Island varieties",html:"<p>Speech differs among Miyako, Ikema, Irabu, Tarama, and smaller communities. A single written or broadcast standard therefore requires political choices about which pronunciation, vocabulary, and grammar receive public authority.</p>"},
      {id:"history",title:"Kingdom and island administration",html:"<p>Miyako entered the Ryukyu Kingdom's tribute and administrative system while retaining local elites and institutions. Satsuma influence and later Japanese prefectural rule added further layers without eliminating local identity.</p>"},
      {id:"shift",title:"Japanese-language shift",html:"<p>Schools stigmatize island speech and teach standard Japanese. Migration to Naha, Taiwan, the home islands, and Pacific territories rewards Japanese fluency. Returning workers and soldiers introduce new vocabulary while children increasingly cease acquiring full Miyakoan grammar.</p>"},
      {id:"continuity",title:"No occupation intermission",html:"<p>The absence of American rule leaves Japanese education and administration uninterrupted. It also spares island institutions from a separate occupation bureaucracy, making the later preservation movement a reform within imperial Japan rather than a recovery after reversion.</p>"},
      {id:"preservation",title:"Documentation and teaching",html:"<p>Field recordings, dictionaries, community texts, songs, ritual speech, school clubs, and comparative research preserve multiple varieties. Teachers must choose between local authenticity and materials usable across the entire Miyako group.</p>"},
      {id:"1985",title:"Status in 1985",html:"<p>By 1985 Miyakoan is severely endangered, with fluent traditional speech concentrated among older islanders. Public performance and heritage visibility exceed everyday child transmission. The archive leaves exact official terminology and counts open.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"ryukyuan-languages.html",kicker:"Language family",label:"Ryukyuan Languages"},{href:"okinawa-prefecture.html",kicker:"Prefecture",label:"Okinawa Prefecture"},{href:"yaeyama-language.html",kicker:"Closest major relative",label:"Yaeyama Language"},{href:"yonaguni-language.html",kicker:"Southern relative",label:"Yonaguni Language"},{href:"japanese-preservationism.html",kicker:"Revival policy",label:"Japanese Preservationism"}],
    facts:[["Family","Japonic"],["Branch","Southern Ryukyuan"],["Principal islands","Miyako group"],["Major varieties","Miyako, Ikema, Irabu, and Tarama"],["Closest major relative","Yaeyama"],["Dominant contact language","Japanese"],["American occupation","None"],["Status in 1985","Severely endangered; exact designation open"]],
    sources:[{href:"https://repository.ninjal.ac.jp/record/2535/files/Research_Report_on_Miyako_eng.pdf",label:"National Institute for Japanese Language and Linguistics — Miyako Ryukyuan report"},{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miyakoan_language",label:"Linguistic reference — Miyakoan language"}]
  }),

  "yaeyama-language":okinawaRyukyuArticle({
    title:"Yaeyama language",category:"Languages",infoboxKicker:"Southern Ryukyuan language of the Yaeyama Islands",
    lead:"Yaeyama is the Southern Ryukyuan language of Ishigaki, Iriomote, Taketomi, Hateruma, Hatoma, and most of the Yaeyama Islands apart from Yonaguni. It comprises sharply local island and village varieties whose speakers share a regional identity but no universally inherited spoken standard.",
    canon:"Yaeyama's Southern Ryukyuan classification, island distribution, exclusion of Yonaguni, local diversity, Japanese displacement, and cultural traditions are historical baseline. Continued Japanese administration, Taiwan-facing imperial geography, and preservationist intervention follow setting canon. Exact 1985 speaker counts, dialect boundaries, orthography, schooling, and classification remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"archipelago",title:"Yaeyama archipelago",html:"<p>The language is distributed across the southwestern islands centered economically and administratively on Ishigaki. Iriomote, Taketomi, Hateruma, Hatoma, and other communities maintain varieties shaped by small populations, maritime travel, and strong village identity.</p>"},
      {id:"classification",title:"Classification",html:"<p>Yaeyama belongs to Southern Ryukyuan and is related to Miyakoan and Yonaguni. <a href='yonaguni-language.html'>Yonaguni</a> is treated separately because of its distinct linguistic development despite belonging geographically to the Yaeyama Islands.</p>"},
      {id:"variation",title:"Island and village variation",html:"<p>Mutual comprehension varies, and the most visible Ishigaki forms do not exhaust the language. Migration into the regional center can replace a small-island variety first with Ishigaki speech and then with Japanese.</p>"},
      {id:"kingdom",title:"Kingdom-era administration",html:"<p>The Ryukyu court governed Yaeyama through officials, tribute, maritime routes, and local intermediaries. Taxation, forced production, movement controls, ritual, and resistance left distinct island memories within the wider kingdom.</p>"},
      {id:"writing",title:"Writing and record",html:"<p>Official records used written traditions connected to the kingdom and Japan rather than a common vernacular orthography. Local mnemonic and pictographic practices, including Kaidā signs in parts of the southern islands, remain important subjects of cultural history.</p>"},
      {id:"shift",title:"Shift to Japanese",html:"<p>Schools, administration, radio, transport, military service, and commercial concentration in Ishigaki make Japanese the common public language. Children use regional Japanese while traditional Yaeyama remains strongest among elders, ritual specialists, and performance communities.</p>"},
      {id:"preservation",title:"Preserving diversity",html:"<p>Recordings, dictionaries, song archives, festivals, local history, school clubs, and village teaching preserve the language. A single Yaeyama standard may improve teaching efficiency while accelerating the disappearance of the smallest varieties.</p>"},
      {id:"1985",title:"Status in 1985",html:"<p>By 1985 Yaeyama is severely endangered and fragmented by island. Strategic proximity to Taiwan increases Japanese administrative and transport presence without turning Taiwanese speech into the principal replacement language.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"ryukyuan-languages.html",kicker:"Language family",label:"Ryukyuan Languages"},{href:"miyakoan-language.html",kicker:"Eastern relative",label:"Miyakoan Language"},{href:"yonaguni-language.html",kicker:"Western neighbor",label:"Yonaguni Language"},{href:"okinawa-prefecture.html",kicker:"Administration",label:"Okinawa Prefecture"},{href:"taiwan.html",kicker:"Nearest major region",label:"Taiwan"}],
    facts:[["Family","Japonic"],["Branch","Southern Ryukyuan"],["Principal center","Ishigaki"],["Other communities","Iriomote, Taketomi, Hateruma, Hatoma, and neighboring islands"],["Geographic exception","Yonaguni has a separate language"],["Dominant contact language","Japanese"],["Historical writing interest","Kaidā signs and official Japanese/Ryukyuan records"],["Status in 1985","Severely endangered; exact counts open"]],
    sources:[{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yaeyama_language",label:"Linguistic reference — Yaeyama language"},{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yaeyama_Islands",label:"Geographical reference — Yaeyama Islands"}]
  }),

  "yonaguni-language":okinawaRyukyuArticle({
    title:"Yonaguni language",category:"Languages",infoboxKicker:"Southern Ryukyuan language of Dunan",
    lead:"Yonaguni is the Southern Ryukyuan language of Yonaguni Island, known locally as Dunan. Spoken at Japan's westernmost inhabited edge within sighting and trading distance of Taiwan, it is linguistically distinct from Yaeyama and carries vocabulary shaped by an unusually small, maritime, and strategically exposed community.",
    canon:"Yonaguni's Dunan endonym, Southern Ryukyuan classification, island location, distinction from Yaeyama, small speech community, and severe real-world endangerment are baseline. Japanese control of Taiwan, absence of American occupation, and mature imperial frontier administration alter contact and strategic history. Exact speaker count, orthography, teaching, and 1985 designation remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"dunan",title:"Dunan and Yonaguni",html:"<p>Yonaguni is the Japanese and international island name; Dunan is the local language's name for the island. The language's endonym, place-names, oral history, and settlement vocabulary preserve a local geography not fully represented by national cartography.</p>"},
      {id:"classification",title:"Classification",html:"<p>Yonaguni belongs to Southern Ryukyuan and is often grouped near Yaeyama in a Macro-Yaeyama relationship. It remains sufficiently distinct to be treated as a separate language rather than an ordinary Yaeyama village variety.</p>"},
      {id:"island",title:"A small western community",html:"<p>The settlements of Sonai, Kubura, and Higawa occupy a compact island between the East China Sea and Philippine Sea. Small population, fishing, farming, typhoons, migration, and reliance on shipping make language transmission vulnerable to a few demographic changes.</p>"},
      {id:"taiwan",title:"Taiwan-facing geography",html:"<p>Taiwan lies roughly a hundred kilometers away and is Japanese territory in the setting. Shipping, weather, fishing, radio, administration, and family movement bind Yonaguni more closely to Taiwan than national maps alone suggest, while Japanese remains the common institutional language on both sides.</p>"},
      {id:"strategy",title:"Frontier administration",html:"<p>Observation posts, weather services, coast patrol, aviation, navigation aids, and communications give the island strategic importance. Rotating personnel and national infrastructure increase Japanese use without necessarily building a large permanent civilian population.</p>"},
      {id:"shift",title:"Language shift",html:"<p>School and public employment require Japanese. Young people leave for Ishigaki, Naha, Taiwan, or the home islands and return with Japanese-speaking households. A language with few speakers can lose an age cohort quickly even without a formal prohibition.</p>"},
      {id:"preservation",title:"Documentation and revival",html:"<p>Recordings, dictionaries, place-name surveys, songs, ritual vocabulary, school clubs, and return visits by emigrant families support preservation. Materials must serve local speakers rather than treating the language only as evidence for historical Japonic linguistics.</p>"},
      {id:"1985",title:"Status in 1985",html:"<p>By 1985 Yonaguni is among Japan's most endangered regional languages. Strategic investment has improved transport and services while increasing Japanese-language contact. The language retains high symbolic value but weak child transmission.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"yaeyama-language.html",kicker:"Nearest regional language",label:"Yaeyama Language"},{href:"ryukyuan-languages.html",kicker:"Language family",label:"Ryukyuan Languages"},{href:"okinawa-prefecture.html",kicker:"Administration",label:"Okinawa Prefecture"},{href:"taiwan.html",kicker:"Western neighbor",label:"Taiwan"},{href:"japanese-preservationism.html",kicker:"Revival policy",label:"Japanese Preservationism"}],
    facts:[["Local island name","Dunan"],["Family","Japonic"],["Branch","Southern Ryukyuan"],["Region","Yonaguni Island"],["Principal settlements","Sonai, Kubura, and Higawa"],["Nearest major region","Taiwan"],["Dominant contact language","Japanese"],["Status in 1985","Severely endangered; exact speaker count open"]],
    sources:[{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yonaguni_language",label:"Linguistic reference — Yonaguni language"},{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yonaguni",label:"Geographical reference — Yonaguni Island"}]
  })
});

const addOkinawaRyukyuRelated = (slug, links) => {
  const article = window.deepArticles[slug];
  if (!article) return;
  article.related = article.related || [];
  for (const link of links) {
    if (!article.related.some(existing => existing.href === link.href)) article.related.push(link);
  }
};

